{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_index","statement":"The Quick Start page introduces about 80% of the React concepts used on a daily basis.","source_span":"This page will give you an introduction to 80% of the React concepts that you will use on a daily basis."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_index","statement":"React apps are made out of components.","source_span":"React apps are made out of *components*."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_index","statement":"React components are JavaScript functions that return markup.","source_span":"React components are JavaScript functions that return markup"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_index","statement":"React component names must always start with a capital letter, while HTML tags must be lowercase.","source_span":"React component names must always start with a capital letter, while HTML tags must be lowercase."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_index","statement":"The markup syntax used in React examples is called JSX.","source_span":"The markup syntax you've seen above is called *JSX*."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_index","statement":"JSX is stricter than HTML and requires closing tags like <br />.","source_span":"JSX is stricter than HTML. You have to close tags like `<br />`."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_index","statement":"In React, a CSS class is specified using the className attribute.","source_span":"In React, you specify a CSS class with `className`."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_index","statement":"React has no special syntax for writing conditions; regular JavaScript techniques (e.g. if statements) are used instead.","source_span":"In React, there is no special syntax for writing conditions."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_index","statement":"Unlike the if statement, the conditional (?) operator works directly inside JSX.","source_span":"Unlike `if`, it works inside JSX."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_index","statement":"React uses the JavaScript array map() function to render lists of components.","source_span":"array `map()` function"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_index","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What percentage of daily-use React concepts does the Quick Start page introduce?","answer":"It introduces about 80% of the React concepts used on a daily basis."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_index","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What are React apps made out of, according to the Quick Start guide?","answer":"React apps are made out of components."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_index","fact_refs":[3],"question":"How must React component names be capitalized, and how does this differ from HTML tags?","answer":"React component names must always start with a capital letter, while HTML tags must be lowercase."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_index","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What is the name given to the markup syntax used in React component examples?","answer":"It is called JSX."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_index","fact_refs":[6],"question":"How do you specify a CSS class on an element in React JSX?","answer":"You use the className attribute, e.g. className=\"avatar\"."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_index","fact_refs":[9],"question":"What JavaScript function does React rely on to render lists of components?","answer":"React relies on the JavaScript array map() function to transform an array into a list of components."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_javascript-in-jsx-with-curly-braces","statement":"String attributes passed to JSX are written in single or double quotes.","source_span":"When you want to pass a string attribute to JSX, you put it in single or double quotes:"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_javascript-in-jsx-with-curly-braces","statement":"To dynamically specify an attribute value from JavaScript, you replace the quotes with curly braces.","source_span":"use a value from JavaScript by replacing `\"` and `\"` with `{` and `}`"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_javascript-in-jsx-with-curly-braces","statement":"Curly braces can be used as text directly inside a JSX tag, e.g. <h1>{name}'s To Do List</h1>.","source_span":"directly inside a JSX tag: `<h1>{name}'s To Do List</h1>` works"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_javascript-in-jsx-with-curly-braces","statement":"Curly braces can be used as attributes immediately following the = sign; src={avatar} reads the avatar variable, while src=\"{avatar}\" passes the literal string \"{avatar}\".","source_span":"`src={avatar}` will read the `avatar` variable, but `src=\"{avatar}\"` will pass the string `\"{avatar}\"`."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_javascript-in-jsx-with-curly-braces","statement":"To pass a JavaScript object as a JSX prop, you must wrap the object in another pair of curly braces (e.g. person={{ name: \"Hedy Lamarr\", inventions: 5 }}).","source_span":"to pass a JS object in JSX, you must wrap the object in another pair of curly braces"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_javascript-in-jsx-with-curly-braces","statement":"Inline style properties in JSX are written in camelCase (e.g. backgroundColor instead of background-color).","source_span":"Inline `style` properties are written in camelCase."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_javascript-in-jsx-with-curly-braces","statement":"In the example, the person object has name 'Gregorio Y. Zara' and a theme object with backgroundColor 'black' and color 'pink'.","source_span":"name: 'Gregorio Y. Zara',\n  theme: {\n    backgroundColor: 'black',\n    color: 'pink'\n  }"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_javascript-in-jsx-with-curly-braces","statement":"The double-curly {{ }} syntax in JSX is not special syntax — it is a JavaScript object literal placed inside JSX's curly braces.","source_span":"it's nothing more than an object inside the JSX curlies!"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_javascript-in-jsx-with-curly-braces","fact_refs":[0],"question":"How do you pass a static string attribute to a JSX element?","answer":"You put it in single or double quotes, e.g. alt=\"Gregorio Y. Zara\"."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_javascript-in-jsx-with-curly-braces","fact_refs":[2,3],"question":"What are the two valid ways to use curly braces inside JSX?","answer":"As text directly inside a JSX tag (e.g. <h1>{name}'s To Do List</h1>), or as an attribute value immediately following the = sign (e.g. src={avatar})."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_javascript-in-jsx-with-curly-braces","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What is the difference between writing src={avatar} and src=\"{avatar}\" in JSX?","answer":"src={avatar} reads the value of the JavaScript variable avatar, whereas src=\"{avatar}\" passes the literal string \"{avatar}\"."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_javascript-in-jsx-with-curly-braces","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How do you pass a JavaScript object as a prop in JSX, such as person={{ name: \"Hedy Lamarr\", inventions: 5 }}?","answer":"You wrap the object in a second pair of curly braces, since the outer braces enter JavaScript and the inner braces form the object literal."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_javascript-in-jsx-with-curly-braces","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What casing convention do inline style property names use in JSX, e.g. for background-color?","answer":"They use camelCase, so background-color becomes backgroundColor."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_javascript-in-jsx-with-curly-braces","fact_refs":[6],"question":"In the example person object, what name and theme colors are defined?","answer":"The person object has name 'Gregorio Y. Zara' and a theme with backgroundColor 'black' and color 'pink'."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_javascript-in-jsx-with-curly-braces","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What does the {{ }} double-curly notation actually represent in JSX?","answer":"It is not special syntax — it's just a JavaScript object literal placed inside JSX's regular curly braces."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_keeping-components-pure","statement":"A pure function does not change any objects or variables that existed before it was called.","source_span":"It does not change any objects or variables that existed before it was called."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_keeping-components-pure","statement":"A pure function, given the same inputs, should always return the same result.","source_span":"Given the same inputs, a pure function should always return the same result."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_keeping-components-pure","statement":"React assumes that every component you write is a pure function.","source_span":"React assumes that every component you write is a pure function."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_keeping-components-pure","statement":"The example pure function double(number) returns 6 when passed 3, always.","source_span":"If you pass it `3`, it will return `6`. Always."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_keeping-components-pure","statement":"Passing drinkers={2} to the Recipe component always returns JSX containing '2 cups of water'.","source_span":"When you pass `drinkers={2}` to `Recipe`, it will return JSX containing `2 cups of water`. Always."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_keeping-components-pure","statement":"Passing drinkers={4} to the Recipe component always returns JSX containing '4 cups of water'.","source_span":"If you pass `drinkers={4}`, it will return JSX containing `4 cups of water`. Always."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_keeping-components-pure","statement":"The impure Cup example component mutates an external guest variable during render using the line guest = guest + 1.","source_span":"guest = guest + 1;"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_keeping-components-pure","statement":"React's Strict Mode calls each component's function twice during development to help find impure components.","source_span":"React offers a \"Strict Mode\" in which it calls each component's function twice during development."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_keeping-components-pure","statement":"Because of the double call under Strict Mode, the impure example displayed 'Guest #2', 'Guest #4', and 'Guest #6' instead of 'Guest #1', 'Guest #2', and 'Guest #3'.","source_span":"the original example displayed \"Guest #2\", \"Guest #4\", and \"Guest #6\" instead of \"Guest #1\", \"Guest #2\", and \"Guest #3\"."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_keeping-components-pure","statement":"The fix for the impure Cup component is to pass guest as a prop instead of reading/writing an external variable.","source_span":"passing `guest` as a prop instead"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_keeping-components-pure","statement":"Props, state, and context are the three kinds of inputs a component can read while rendering, and they should always be treated as read-only.","source_span":"You should always treat these inputs as read-only."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_keeping-components-pure","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What two characteristics define a pure function according to the React docs on keeping components pure?","answer":"A pure function does not change any objects or variables that existed before it was called, and given the same inputs it always returns the same result."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_keeping-components-pure","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What does React assume about every component you write?","answer":"React assumes that every component you write is a pure function."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_keeping-components-pure","fact_refs":[3],"question":"In the pure function example, what does double(3) return, and why is this guaranteed?","answer":"It returns 6, always, because double is a pure function that gives the same output for the same input every time."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_keeping-components-pure","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What JSX text does the Recipe component always return when drinkers is set to 4?","answer":"It always returns JSX containing '4 cups of water'."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_keeping-components-pure","fact_refs":[6,7,8],"question":"Why did the impure Cup example show 'Guest #2', 'Guest #4', and 'Guest #6' instead of 'Guest #1', '#2', '#3'?","answer":"The component mutated an external guest variable via guest = guest + 1 during render, and because React's Strict Mode calls each component function twice in development, the count was doubled."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_keeping-components-pure","fact_refs":[9],"question":"How was the impure Cup component fixed?","answer":"By passing guest as a prop instead of reading and writing a variable declared outside the component."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_keeping-components-pure","fact_refs":[10],"question":"What three kinds of inputs can a React component read while rendering, and how should they be treated?","answer":"Props, state, and context — all three should always be treated as read-only."}
